    "author": "hwchase17",
    "how": "Reconstructed from the tweet and the many summaries relayed (the article itself could not be read from the session). 1) Write down what is in your best people's heads: processes as skills (step-by-step procedure sheets the agent loads when needed), how the business works in one linked wiki, and where the data lives and how to access it (tools = functions the agent can call). 2) Agents flag what is missing or wrong while they work. 3) A curator agent goes through those flags and the traces (the detailed log of what the agent did) every night and fixes the source; it is the only agent that edits the wiki, because task agents edit it badly. 4) Skill changes often need human sign-off (HITL, hum…",
    "links": [
     {
      "label": "Site",
      "url": "https://x.com/i/article/2124180107669766144"
     }
    ],
    "tag": "research",
    "title": "Knowledge Factory (LangChain): the knowledge loop that makes business agents improve",
    "try": "Free: it is a pattern, not a product. Version 0: a folder of Markdown files (processes, glossary, where the data lives) plus an instruction to your agent: at the end of each task, list what you were missing.",
    "tweet_url": "https://x.com/hwchase17/status/2124181216719151585",
    "what": "An article by Harrison Chase (LangChain CEO) describing how all their internal agents (GTM, content, LangSmith Engine) learn continuously. Put simply: permanent onboarding for your agents, like a company wiki that updates itself.",
    "who": "Anyone trying to get agents to do real work in a company. The message: the model is rarely the problem, the agent just does not know how your company works. Many field reports in the RTs: Palm built one because different sources gave different values for the same fact; a team applies it to voice agents (knowledge gaps bubble up, humans answer by reviewing the call); another says background agents already make up 20 to 50% of their runs."
   },
   {
    "accounts": [
     "sqs"
    ],
    "author": "sqs",
    "how": "Puck is Amp's voice assistant. Quinn Slack (CEO) also trained a local wake-word model (\"Hey Puck\", like \"Hey Siri\"): Amp generated synthetic data, used 77.2 hours of his own dictation recordings (no one else's data) and wrote native Swift/Kotlin/Web implementations. Local means the model runs on your device, so nothing is sent to the server until you say the keyword (low latency plus privacy). He plans to queue things you need to look at and show them at a red light. Amp already shows up in CarPlay as a call.",
    "links": [
     {
      "label": "Site",
      "url": "https://ampcode.com/news/puck-in-the-car"
     }
    ],
    "tag": "paid",
    "title": "Amp Puck in the car: drive your coding agent by voice",
    "try": "Available in Amp (paid, pay-as-you-go); the wake word is coming soon. Site not reachable from the session, limited details.",
    "tweet_url": "https://x.com/sqs/status/2124216212129534454",
    "what": "Amp (Sourcegraph's coding agent, a cousin of Claude Code / Codex) adds a car mode: you talk to your agent, it answers by voice and only shows a big picture or a button now and then. Put simply: a Siri for your coding agent.",
    "who": "Shows where agent interfaces are heading: less screen, more asynchronous delegation. Also a nice case of an agent building its own ML component end to end."
   },
